// KNOWN FAILURE: returning a struct INSIDE A TUPLE loses field mutations.
//
// Expected: name=t0 out=3
// Actual:   name=t0 out=1     (both backends, identically)
//
// PARTIALLY FIXED. Was out=0 with 2 double-frees; the temp-drop pass no
// longer releases a struct field-read temp when the source struct escapes
// the statement inside a tuple (see the guard in drop_unescaped_str_temps).
// One accumulation path remains: each iteration returns a struct carrying
// only its own push, not the running total, so a second release is still
// unbalanced somewhere in the copy/dup chain. 1 double-free left.
//
// `store` pushes into `np.out` and returns `(np, len)`. The caller rebinds
// `p` from the tuple, so the pushes must be visible. They are not: the
// returned struct's array field comes back empty.
//
// Returning the struct DIRECTLY is correct -- change `store` to
// `-> P { ... return np }` and the same program prints out=3. Only the
// tuple-wrapped return loses the state, which makes this an inconsistency
// between two spellings of the same thing, not the documented value-copy
// semantics.
//
// It also double-frees: KRYOS_FREE_DIAG=1 reports 2 str DOUBLE-FREEs here,
// and the same shape at self-host scale segfaults.
//
// WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS: it is the shape the self-host parser
// is built on. Every `p_*` function in compiler/self-host/stage1_mini_parser.kry
// returns `(Parser, i64)` and threads the parser through it. That program has
// 7 remaining double-frees and dies with a segfault (`kryos run`), which is
// the only red CI job. On master the same corruption surfaced as a bogus
// `parse_int: invalid numeric input: '}'` panic -- under KRYOS_FREE_DIAG=1
// (no deallocation) master runs it to completion, proving the "parse error"
// was memory corruption, not parse logic.
//
// Reduction path, for anyone picking this up (each step verified):
//   field read + advance                          clean
//   + struct reassigned in a loop                 clean
//   + push into a struct's array field            clean
//   + return the struct in a TUPLE                BREAKS  <- this file
//
// TRACE OF THE REMAINING HALF (instrument store() and the loop with println):
//
//   iter 0 before: len=0
//     store: in len=0 / after copy len=0 / after push len=1
//   iter 0 returned k=1  p2 len=1
//   iter 0 after assign: len=1          <- correct
//   iter 1 before: len=1
//     store: in len=1 / after copy len=1 / after push len=2
//   iter 1 returned k=2  p2 len=2       <- the callee is CORRECT
//   iter 1 after assign: len=0          <- LOST HERE, on `p = p2`
//
// So the callee builds the right value and the tuple carries it out intact.
// The loss is at `p = p2`: a struct assigned from another local aliases that
// local's heap fields and takes no reference to them (retain_for_ty is None
// for Struct, and emit_param_source_retain only fires for params). `p2` is a
// tuple-destructured NAMED local, so it gets a scope-end Drop at the end of
// the loop body, which frees the array `p` now points at. Iteration 0 survives
// only because the array was still empty when it was freed.
//
// RULED OUT (each tested):
//   * the guarded struct reassignment release
//     (release_struct_heap_fields_if_ne) -- disabling it entirely does not
//     change the result, so the pointer-equality guard is not the problem
//   * adding a retain in the `param_src` branch of the reassignment path --
//     no effect, that branch is not the one taken here; find the branch that
//     actually fires for `p = p2` first (retain_container_src may be taking it)
//
// Acceptance: this file prints `name=t0 out=3` on BOTH backends, and
// `KRYOS_FREE_DIAG=1` reports 0 double-frees. Then re-check
// stage1_mini_parser.kry reaches rc=0.

struct Tok { text: str, kind: i64 }
struct P { toks: [Tok], out: [str], pos: i64 }

fn cur_text(p: P) -> str {
    let t = p.toks[p.pos]
    return t.text
}

fn store(p: P, s: str) -> (P, i64) {
    let mut np = p
    push(np.out, s)
    return (np, len(np.out))
}

@capabilities(all)
fn main() {
    let mut toks: [Tok] = []
    let mut i = 0
    while i < 4 {
        toks = push(toks, Tok { text: "t" + to_string(i), kind: i })
        i = i + 1
    }
    let mut p = P { toks: toks, out: [], pos: 0 }

    // a retained field read held live across the churn below
    let name = cur_text(p)

    let mut n = 0
    while n < 3 {
        let (p2, _) = store(p, "k")
        p = p2
        n = n + 1
    }

    println("name=" + name + " out=" + to_string(len(p.out)))
}
